## The Width Floor at 3ct — Why Length Dominates

**The Width Floor is the point where marquise width stops growing fast enough to match buyer expectations.** At 3ct, length is 15 mm but width is only 7.5 mm — the same width as a 1.75 carat round diamond. Buyers who expect "3 carat" to look like a 3ct round on the hand get a surprise: the marquise looks longer, not wider.

### Width Comparison vs Round at the Same Carat

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Spec</th><th>3ct Marquise</th><th>3ct Round</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Length (mm)</strong></td><td>15.0</td><td>9.3</td><td>Marquise is 61% longer than round diameter.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Width (mm)</strong></td><td>7.5</td><td>9.3</td><td>Marquise is 1.8 mm narrower than round. Visible gap.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Face-up area (mm²)</strong></td><td>88.4</td><td>67.9</td><td>Marquise wins area by 30%.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Side-to-side finger coverage (size 6)</strong></td><td>45%</td><td>56%</td><td>Round wins side-to-side width.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lengthwise coverage</strong></td><td>91%</td><td>56%</td><td>Marquise wins length coverage by huge margin.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** 3ct marquise wins area and length but loses side-to-side width to 3ct round. If your partner wears rings in a lengthwise orientation (standard), marquise wins. If they prefer the "wide stone" look (common on size 7+ fingers with established jewelry), round wins. The Width Floor is why this choice matters more at 3ct than at smaller tiers.

## The $50,868 Lab Arbitrage Window at 3ct

The gap between natural 3ct ($54,687 floor) and lab 3ct ($3,819 floor) is **$50,868**. That is more than 14x the price for a stone that is physically identical on the hand. The arbitrage window at 3ct is the biggest on the entire marquise chart in absolute dollar terms.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Spec</th><th>Natural (3ct)</th><th>Lab (3ct)</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Floor price</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28651376?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$54,687</a></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30343375?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$3,819</a></td><td>Lab costs 7% of natural at the floor.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Color (floor stone)</strong></td><td>G</td><td>E</td><td>Lab wins by 2 colour grades.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Clarity (floor stone)</strong></td><td>VS2</td><td>VS1</td><td>Lab wins by 1 clarity grade.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Cut label</strong></td><td>Ideal</td><td>Ideal</td><td>Tied on cut label.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Face-up appearance</strong></td><td>15 × 7.5 mm</td><td>15 × 7.5 mm</td><td>Identical. No difference on the hand.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Natural premium</strong></td><td>+$50,868</td><td>—</td><td>Enough for a car, kitchen renovation, or 10 years of family vacations.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** The $50,868 arbitrage window at 3ct is where sentiment pays its highest premium. For buyers who care about "natural + GIA" the premium is defensible. For every other buyer, lab is the honest answer at this tier.

